<<2>> Give me a combination of a sequence completion and an arithmetic exercise for a 6th grade student learning mathematics. It should be an exercise involving sequence completion.
<<2>> Given the sequence: 2, 6, 12, 20, 30,..., what is the next number in the sequence? Explain how you found your answer.  

\begin{equation*}
   \text{Let } a_n \text{ be the nth term of the sequence. Then:}  
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
   a_1 = 2, a_2 = 6, a_3 = 12, a_4 = 20, a_5 = 30, \ldots
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
   a_{n+1} = a_n + 4, \text{ for } n \geq 1.
\end{equation*}  

\begin{equation*}
   \text{Thus, the next number in the sequence is:}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
   a_6 = a_5 + 4 = 30 + 4 = \boxed{42}.
\end{equation*}
