<<16>> Give me an algebra exercise for a 6th grade student learning mathematics. It should be an exercise on evaluating expressions. It should have multiple steps. There should be marks/points assigned to the question. It should be a with Answer.
<<16>> Find the value of each of the following expressions when \( x = 5 \). (2 marks each)

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item \( 2x + 3 = \_\_\_ \)
\item \( 2x - 3 = \_\_\_ \)
\item \( 3x + 3 = \_\_\_ \)
\item \( 5x - 2 = \_\_\_ \)
\item \( 3x - 4 = \_\_\_ \)
\item \( 4x + 5 = \_\_\_ \)
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Answers}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item \( 2(5) + 3 = 13 \)
\item \( 2(5) - 3 = 7 \)
\item \( 3(5) + 3 = 18 \)
\item \( 5(5) - 2 = 23 \)
\item \( 3(5) - 4 = 11 \)
\item \( 4(5) + 5 = 25 \)
\end{enumerate}
<<17>> Give me an algebra exercise for a 6th grade student learning mathematics. It should be an exercise on algebraic expressions. It should have multiple steps.
<<17>> There are 8 children. \( v \) of them are boys.

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item Express the number of girls in terms of \( v \).
\item If \( v = 3 \), find the number of girls.
\item If \( v = 5 \), find the number of girls.
\end{enumerate}
<<18>> Give me a combination of an investigation and a partner activity for a 1st grade student learning science. It should have a subsection for materials. It should be an exercise on making things go faster or slower.
<<18>> \paragraph{Investigation} How can you make a toy change speed or stop?
\begin{enumerate}
\item Work with a partner.
\item Take turns to push a toy along a smooth, flat surface.
\item Measure how far the toy travels.
\item How can you make the toy change speed or stop?
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Toy
\item Measuring tape
\end{itemize}
<<19>> Give me a combination of an investigation exercise, a partner activity, and a question for a 2nd grade student learning science. It should have a subsection for materials. It should be an exercise on light sources.
<<19>> \paragraph{Investigation} How can you investigate a source of light?
\begin{enumerate}
\item Work with a partner.
\item Look around the room and find a source of light.
\item Try to find out what the source of light is.
\item Can you find any clues?
\item Can you find any words that give you a clue?
\item Tell the class what you found out.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item A source of light (e.g., a lamp or the Sun)
\end{itemize}
