<<37>> Give me an investigation exercise for a 4th grade student learning science. It should be an exercise on sound. It should be an exercise on changing pitch.
<<37>> Investigate how to change the pitch of a drum. You will need:
\begin{itemize}
\item A drum or a drumhead
\item Some rice or sand
\item A container to put the rice or sand in
\item A spoon or stick to bang the drum
\item Some water
\end{itemize}
<<38>> Give me an investigation exercise for a 1st grade student learning science. It should be an exercise on eating and drinking. It should be an exercise on healthy foods.
<<38>> \paragraph{Investigation} Sorting food into healthy and unhealthy groups.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Look at the pictures of different foods.
\item Sort the pictures into two groups: healthy and unhealthy.
\item Display your groups for the class to see.
\item Look at what other learners have done.
\end{enumerate}
<<39>> Give me an investigation exercise for a 5th grade student learning science. It should be an exercise on measuring light intensity.
<<39>> \paragraph{Investigation} Measuring light intensity
\begin{itemize}
\item Handle the light meter carefully and take readings in different places in the room.
\item What units do you think the readings will be in?
\item Record your readings in a table.
\item Where is the light the brightest? Where is it the dimmest?
\end{itemize}
