<<81>> Give me a combination of a word problem and an arithmetic exercise for a 3rd grade student learning mathematics. It should be an exercise on cost comparison.
<<81>> The cost of a motor cycle is \$3000. The cost of a car is \$60,000. How many motor cycles can be bought with the money that can buy one car? How much money will be left? Show your calculation. Explain your answer. Remember to show your method.  

\begin{equation*}
\text{Number of motor cycles} = \frac{\text{Cost of the car}}{\text{Cost of the motor cycle}} = \frac{60,000}{3000} = \_\_\_
\end{equation*}  
\begin{equation*}
\text{Amount of money left} = \text{Cost of the car} - (\text{Number of motor cycles} \times \text{Cost of the motor cycle}) = \_\_\_
\end{equation*}  

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item How many motor cycles can be bought with the money that can buy one car? 
\item How much money will be left? 
\end{enumerate}
