<<126>> Give me a short answer exercise for a 6th grade student learning history. It should be an exercise on the Great Fire of London.
<<126>> Write a brief explanation of the Great Fire of London. Include the date when the fire started and the areas of London that were destroyed. Also, describe how and why London was rebuilt. Include details of the people involved in the rebuilding and the new types of building constructed. Finally, explain why the Great Fire was an important turning point in the history of London.  

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item When did the Great Fire of London start?  
\item What areas of London were destroyed in the fire?  
\item How and why was London rebuilt after the fire?  
\item Who were the people involved in the rebuilding?  
\item What new types of building were constructed?  
\item Why was the Great Fire an important turning point in the history of London?  
\end{enumerate}  



\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item 2 September 1666
\item The city of London, from the River Thames to the north and from the Tower of London to the west.
\item London was rebuilt after the fire because people needed homes and places to work. The fire had destroyed the wooden buildings and narrow streets. The new buildings were made of brick or stone. Wide, straight streets were built, with open spaces to stop fires spreading.
\item The people involved in the rebuilding were King Charles II, his brother James (the Duke of York), the architect Christopher Wren, Robert Hooke, and the people who lived and worked in London.
\item New types of building constructed included churches, hospitals, and the Royal Greenwich Observatory.
\item The Great Fire was an important turning point in the history of London because it led to the rebuilding of the city. London became a modern city with wide streets and open spaces. The city became less crowded and dirty, with fewer narrow streets and wooden buildings.
\end{enumerate}  



The Great Fire of London started on 2 September 1666. It destroyed the city of London, from the River Thames to the north and from the Tower of London to the west. London was rebuilt after the fire because people needed homes and places to work. The fire had destroyed the wooden buildings and narrow streets. The new buildings were made of brick or stone. Wide, straight streets were built, with open spaces to stop fires spreading. The people involved in the rebuilding were King Charles II, his brother James (the Duke of York), the architect Christopher Wren, Robert Hooke, and the people who lived and worked in London. New types of building constructed included churches, hospitals, and the Royal Greenwich Observatory. The Great Fire was an important turning point in the history of London because it led to the rebuilding of the city. London became a modern city with wide streets and open spaces. The city became less crowded and dirty, with fewer narrow streets and wooden buildings.  



