<<505>> Give me a vocabulary exercise for a 6th grade student learning English.
<<505>> \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item Write a word from the list that fits into each of the sentences below. Each word should have one letter changed from the original word.
    \begin{itemize}
    \item \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_
    \item \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_
    \item \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_
    \item \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_
    \item \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_
    \end{itemize}

    \begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
    \item The \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_ is the person in charge of the library.
    \item I like to \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_ books from the library.
    \item The \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_ in a book tells us who wrote it.
    \item The \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_ of a book tells us what the book is about.
    \item The \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_ in a library is very important.
    \end{enumerate}

\item Now, write each of the original words with two letters changed.
    \begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
    \item \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_
    \item \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_
    \item \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_
    \item \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_
    \item \_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_\_
    \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
<<506>> Give me a group exercise for a 1st grade student learning geography.
<<506>> Work in a group. Look at the map of the world on the wall or in an atlas.  
\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item Find the countries that make up the continents of Asia, Africa and Europe.  
\item Label them on a blank map of the world.  
\item Display your map for the class to see.  
\end{enumerate}
<<507>> Give me a punctuation exercise for a 1st grade student learning English.
<<507>> Add the missing speech marks and other punctuation to each sentence:
\begin{enumerate}
\item I said I like beans
\item Mum said What did you say
\item Dad said I said I like beans
\item I said Dad said I said I like beans
\end{enumerate}
<<508>> Give me a combination of an estimating exercise and a temporal exercise for a 3rd grade student learning mathematics.
<<508>> Estimate how long each activity takes:

\begin{enumerate}[label=\alph*.]
\item Brushing your teeth
\item Having a bath or shower
\item Walking to school
\end{enumerate}
<<509>> Give me a descending order exercise for a 6th grade student learning mathematics.
<<509>> Write these numbers in descending order: 10.8, 10.05, 10.4, 10.2, 10.01.  
