<<520>> Give me a short answer exercise for a 2nd grade student learning computer science.
<<520>> Describe the differences between a desktop and a laptop in your own words. How do they feel to use? How do they look? What are some advantages of each? Which one do you prefer to use? Explain your reasons.  

[STRDGM] A diagram showing a desktop computer on one side, with a separate monitor, keyboard, and mouse, and a laptop on the other side, with a built-in keyboard and touchpad. The desktop is larger and has more components visible, while the laptop is smaller and more compact. [STPDGM]
<<521>> Give me a short answer exercise for a 4th grade student learning computer science.
<<521>> What do you think makes a good file name? Describe your answer in detail. Think about the following points:  
\begin{itemize}
\item how you can identify the content of the file  
\item how you can identify the type of the file  
\item how you can tell when the file was created or modified  
\item how you can tell who created the file.
\end{itemize}  
What do you think is most important? Give reasons for your answer.  
Now think about a file name you would give to a file you are creating. Give an example. Explain how your file name shows the things you think are important.  



\subsection*{Think With Me}
\begin{enumerate}
\item What are the advantages of using file names instead of numbers?  
\item What are the disadvantages of using file names instead of numbers?  
\item What are the advantages of using numbers instead of file names?  
\item What are the disadvantages of using numbers instead of file names?  
\end{enumerate}

\subsection*{Bonus Question}
\begin{enumerate}
\item What would you name a file called 'My first file'? Give reasons for your answer.
\end{enumerate}  



\subsection*{Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item A computer or device with internet access
\end{itemize}  



\subsection*{Prerequisites}
\begin{itemize}
\item Knowledge of how to use a computer or device to create and save files.
\end{itemize}  



\subsection*{Related Research Topics}
\begin{itemize}
\item File naming conventions
\item File organization strategies
\item Data storage and retrieval
\end{itemize}  



\subsection*{Questions for Further Study}
\begin{enumerate}
\item What are some common file naming conventions?  
\item How do different operating systems handle file naming?  
\item What are some strategies for organizing files on a computer or device?  
\item How do search functions work when looking for files?  
\end{enumerate}  



\subsection*{Activities}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Create a new file and give it a name. Experiment with different names and see how they affect your ability to find the file later.
\item Think of a time when you had trouble finding a file on your computer or device. What could you have done differently to make it easier to find?
\item Talk to someone who uses computers for work or school. Ask them about their strategies for naming and organizing files.
\end{enumerate}  



\subsection*{Long Answer Exercise}
Give reasons for your answer to this question: Why is it important to use good file names?  



\subsection*{Reflection Exercise}
What do you think is the most important part of using a computer or device? Give reasons for your answer. Think about the following points:  
\begin{itemize}
\item saving files in the correct place  
\item giving files good names  
\item using folders to organize files  
\item keeping your desktop tidy.
\end{itemize}  



\subsection*{Bonus Activity}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Make a list of your favorite foods or animals. Give each one a good file name.  
\item Create a folder for each one and put it in the correct place.  
\item Make a folder called 'My favorite things'. Put all the folders you made in this folder.  
\item Make a folder called 'Things I like'. Put the folder 'My favorite things' in this folder.  
\item Make a folder called 'School work'. Put the folder 'Things I like' in this folder.
\end{enumerate}  



\subsection*{Activity}
\begin{enumerate}
